{"file_type":"RF_VCB_PROJECT","file_version":2,"title":"","creation_date":1749068803,"last_edit_date":1794784381,"time_worked_on":190471,"layers":["","",""],"assembly":"# Advent of Code 2025, day 3 part 2 (Lobby)\n#\n# THE PUZZLE\n# Each line of the input is a bank of batteries, each battery a digit 1-9. From every bank exactly twelve\n# batteries are switched on; the bank's joltage is the 12-digit number they form, in their order in the\n# bank. The answer is the sum of the largest joltage of every bank.\n#\n# THE IDEA\n# Choosing 12 digits out of a bank of n is the same as dropping n - 12 of them. A larger digit further\n# left always wins, so: go through the bank once and keep the chosen digits on a stack. Before a new\n# digit D goes on the stack, throw away the digits on top of it that are smaller than D, as long as\n# drops are left. The first 12 digits on the stack at the end are the answer for the bank.\n# Example 234234234234278 (15 digits, 3 drops):\n#     2            stack 2\n#     3   drop 2   stack 3              (2 drops left)\n#     4   drop 3   stack 4              (1 drop left)\n#     2            stack 4 2\n#     3   drop 2   stack 4 3            (no drops left: from here every digit is kept)\n#     ...          stack 4 3 4 2 3 4 2 3 4 2 7 8      = 434234234278\n# When a bank never uses all its drops (987654321111111), the stack ends up longer than 12 and the\n# digits beyond the twelfth are the ones dropped.\n#\n# NO BIG NUMBERS\n# A joltage has 12 digits (40 bits) and the sum needs more, but the program never forms such a number.\n# It adds the chosen digits column by column: the first digits of all banks into one cell, the second\n# digits into the next, and so on. Those twelve column sums already are the answer, written with\n# \"digits\" that are too large. At the end the cells are put right from the last one to the first:\n# each keeps its value modulo 10 and hands the rest, divided by 10, to the cell before it. That is the\n# carrying of a written addition. The result cells are used for the column sums from the start.\n#\n# THE WHOLE PROGRAM IN PSEUDO CODE\n#     RESULT[0..14] = 0                         # 15 cells; a bank's 12 digits go to cells 3..14\n#     loop:\n#         END = IN; while input[END] != 0: END = END + 1       # find the end of the bank\n#         if END == IN: break                   # an empty bank: the end of the input\n#         DROPS = (END - IN) - 12\n#         TOP = STACK                           # the stack is empty; TOP is where the next digit goes\n#         while IN != END:\n#             D = input[IN]; IN = IN + 1\n#             while DROPS != 0 and memory[TOP - 1] < D:        # the word below the stack is a 9,\n#                 TOP = TOP - 1; DROPS = DROPS - 1             # so an empty stack never \"has\" a smaller digit\n#             memory[TOP] = D; TOP = TOP + 1\n#         for i = 0..11: RESULT[3 + i] = RESULT[3 + i] + STACK[i]\n#         IN = IN + 1                           # step over the 0\n#     CARRY = 0\n#     for P = 14 down to 0:                     # the carrying\n#         X = RESULT[P] + CARRY\n#         CARRY = 0; while X >= 10: X = X - 10; CARRY = CARRY + 1\n#         RESULT[P] = X\n#     stop\n#\n# THE RESULT, when the CPU stops on the Breakpoint at END_OF_PROGRAM\n#   0x0110-0x011E  the sum as 15 decimal digits, one per word, most significant first\n# (There is no binary form of the sum: the program never builds one.)\n#\n# THE INPUT (rule 1 of RULES.md), at 0x2000\n# One word per battery holding its digit, bank after bank; a word 0 ends each bank, and a second 0 after\n# the last bank ends the input. Built-in limits: a bank has 12 to 256 batteries; at most 7,000 banks\n# (a column sum must stay below 65,536).\n#\n# ONE WORD IS ENOUGH\n# Every value here fits in 16 bits, so each variable is one memory word. The idioms used everywhere\n# (v is a variable's address, below 0x1000):\n#     getA v                   A = the word at v   (lia v; load rgA rgA: lia puts the address in A, load replaces it)\n#     getB v                   B = the word at v\n#     putA v                   the word at v = A   (this uses up B)\n#     if_eq L / if_neq L       compares A with B as they are now\n#     sub; if_nega L           after A - B: taken when A < B\n# A variable that holds an address is a pointer (IN, END, TOP, SRC, DST, P). Reading the word it points\n# at takes one more load: lia 0; lib IN; load rgB rgB; load rgA rgB.\n# What each instruction does exactly is in findings/cpu-instructions.md.\n#\n# MEMORY MAP\n#   0x0110-0x011E  the result           0x1000-  the program\n#   0x0120-0x012B  variables            0x2000-  the input (long: 200 banks of 100 end at 0x6EE8)\n#   0x01FF         the 9 below the stack\n#   0x0200-0x02FF  the stack            0xFF00   where the CPU starts\n\n# ---- The CPU's instructions as symbols (the same table as in day 1) ----\nsymbol nop 0b0000_0000_0000_0000\nsymbol ld  0b0010_0000_0000_0000\nsymbol lia 0b0011_0000_0000_0000\nsymbol lib 0b0100_0000_0000_0000\nsymbol sto 0b0101_0000_0000_0000\n\nmacro  load  { ld  | a? | (b? >> 6) }\nmacro  store { sto | a? | (b? >> 6) }\n\n# ---- Macros for the common operations (the README explains them; a macro cannot use load or store,\n#      so the bodies spell those out). v: a variable, p: a variable holding an address, L: a label ----\nmacro getA     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                                                  # getA v: A = v\nmacro getB     { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6) }                                                  # getB v: B = v\nmacro putA     { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                                                 # putA v: v = A (B is used up)\nmacro putAat   { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6); sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                          # putAat p: the word p points at = A (B is used up)\nmacro inc      { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib 1; sum; lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }      # inc v: v = v + 1 (A and B are used up)\nmacro goto     { jmp; a? }                                                                        # goto L: jump to L\nmacro if_eq    { jmp eq; a? }                                                                     # if_eq L: jump to L when A == B\nmacro if_neq   { jmp neq; a? }                                                                    # if_neq L: jump to L when A != B\nmacro if_carry { jmp carry; a? }                                                                  # if_carry L: jump to L when the last sum or sub set the carry\nmacro if_nega  { jmp nega; a? }                                                                   # if_nega L: jump to L when the last sub went below 0\n\nsymbol sum    0b0110_0100_0000_0000\nsymbol sum_32 0b0110_1100_0000_0000\nsymbol sub    0b0110_0010_0000_0000\nsymbol sub_32 0b0110_1010_0000_0000\nsymbol shf    0b0110_0001_0000_0000\nsymbol shf_32 0b0110_1001_0000_0000\nsymbol and    0b0110_0000_1000_0000\nsymbol and_32 0b0110_1000_1000_0000\nsymbol or     0b0110_0000_0100_0000\nsymbol or_32  0b0110_1000_0100_0000\nsymbol xor    0b0110_0000_0010_0000\nsymbol xor_32 0b0110_1000_0010_0000\nsymbol neg    0b0110_0000_0001_0000\nsymbol neg_32 0b0110_1000_0001_0000\n\nsymbol psh 0b1010_0000_0000_0000\nsymbol pop 0b1011_0000_0000_0000\n\nsymbol jmp  0b1100_0000_0000_0000\nsymbol call 0b1110_0000_0000_0000\nsymbol ret  0b1111_0000_0000_0000\n\nsymbol rgAH 0b1000_0000_0000\nsymbol rgA  0b0100_0000_0000\nsymbol rgBH 0b0010_0000_0000\nsymbol rgB  0b0001_0000_0000\nsymbol rgSP 0b0000_1000_0000\nsymbol rgSB 0b0000_0100_0000\nsymbol non  0\n\nsymbol zero  0b1000_0000_0000\nsymbol carry 0b0100_0000_0000\nsymbol nega  0b0010_0000_0000\nsymbol eq    0b0001_0000_0000\nsymbol neq   0b0000_1000_0000\n\n# ---- Variables (all below 0x1000, so lia/lib can address them) ----\n# The result: 15 cells. RESULT_COLUMNS is the cell for a bank's first digit, RESULT_LAST the last cell.\npointer RESULT 0x0110 0\npointer RESULT_COLUMNS 0x0113 0\npointer RESULT_LAST 0x011E 0\n# The current bank: IN the next digit to read, END the address of the 0 behind the bank,\n# DROPS how many digits may still be thrown away, D the digit just read\npointer IN 0x0120 0x2000\npointer END 0x0121 0\npointer DROPS 0x0122 0\npointer D 0x0123 0\n# TOP: the address where the next digit goes on the stack\npointer TOP 0x0124 0\n# For adding the stack to the result, and for the carrying\npointer SRC 0x0125 0\npointer DST 0x0126 0\npointer P 0x0127 0\npointer X 0x0128 0\npointer CARRY 0x0129 0\n# Constants that do not fit an instruction's place for a number, or are needed in a register\npointer TWELFTH 0x012A 0x020C\npointer FIRST_CELL 0x012B 0x0110\n# The stack of chosen digits, and a 9 just below it that is never smaller than a digit\npointer BELOW_STACK 0x01FF 9\npointer STACK 0x0200 0\n\n# ---- The CPU starts at 0xFF00 ----\norigin 0xFF00\ngoto MAIN\n\norigin 0x1000\n@ MAIN\n# ==== find the end of the next bank ====\n#     END = IN\n#     while input[END] != 0: END = END + 1\n#     if END == IN: goto ALL_BANKS_DONE         # no digit: this 0 ends the input\n@ NEXT_BANK\n\tgetA IN; putA END                                            # END = IN\n@ FIND_END\n\tlia 0; getB END; load rgA rgB                                # A = input[END]\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0, to compare with\n\tif_eq END_FOUND                                              # input[END] == 0\n\tinc END                                                      # END = END + 1\n\tgoto FIND_END\n@ END_FOUND\n\tgetA END; getB IN                                            # A = END, B = IN\n\tif_eq ALL_BANKS_DONE                                         # an empty bank: the input is finished\n\n# ==== start the bank ====\n#     DROPS = (END - IN) - 12                   # A and B still hold END and IN\n#     TOP = STACK                               # an empty stack\n\tsub; lib 12; sub; putA DROPS                                 # DROPS = END - IN - 12\n\tlia STACK; putA TOP                                          # TOP = the address of the stack's first word\n\n# ==== for each digit of the bank ====\n#     D = input[IN]; IN = IN + 1\n@ NEXT_DIGIT\n\tlia 0; getB IN; load rgA rgB                                 # A = input[IN]\n\tputA D                                                       # D = A\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1\n\n# ==== throw away smaller digits on top of the stack ====\n#     while DROPS != 0 and memory[TOP - 1] < D:\n#         TOP = TOP - 1; DROPS = DROPS - 1\n# TOP - 1 is computed in A and then used as the address: load rgA rgA reads the word A points at.\n# With an empty stack that word is the 9 in BELOW_STACK, and 9 < D is never true.\n@ DROP_SMALLER\n\tgetA DROPS; lib 0                                            # A = DROPS, B = 0\n\tif_eq PUSH                                                   # no drops left\n\tgetA TOP; lib 1; sub                                         # A = TOP - 1, the address of the top digit\n\tload rgA rgA                                                 # A = the top digit\n\tgetB D                                                       # B = D\n\tsub                                                          # A - B is negative when the top digit < D\n\tif_nega DROP_ONE\n\tgoto PUSH                                                    # the top digit is not smaller: keep it\n@ DROP_ONE\n\tgetA TOP; lib 1; sub; putA TOP                               # TOP = TOP - 1\n\tgetA DROPS; lib 1; sub; putA DROPS                           # DROPS = DROPS - 1\n\tgoto DROP_SMALLER\n\n# ==== put the digit on the stack ====\n#     memory[TOP] = D; TOP = TOP + 1\n#     if IN != END: goto NEXT_DIGIT\n@ PUSH\n\tgetA D; putAat TOP                                           # memory[TOP] = D   (B holds the address in TOP)\n\tinc TOP                                                      # TOP = TOP + 1\n\tgetA IN; getB END                                            # A = IN, B = END\n\tif_neq NEXT_DIGIT                                            # more digits in this bank\n\n# ==== add the bank's twelve digits to the column sums ====\n#     SRC = STACK; DST = RESULT_COLUMNS\n#     repeat: memory[DST] = memory[DST] + memory[SRC]; SRC = SRC + 1; DST = DST + 1\n#     until SRC == TWELFTH                      # the address of the stack's 13th word\n# Reading memory[DST] into B takes two loads into B: first the address, then the word there.\n\tlia STACK; putA SRC                                          # SRC = the address of the stack's first word\n\tlia RESULT_COLUMNS; putA DST                                 # DST = the address of the first column's cell\n@ ADD_COLUMN\n\tlia 0; getB SRC; load rgA rgB                                # A = memory[SRC], a chosen digit\n\tgetB DST; load rgB rgB                                       # B = memory[DST], the column sum so far\n\tsum                                                          # A = column sum + digit\n\tputAat DST                                                   # memory[DST] = A\n\tinc DST                                                      # DST = DST + 1\n\tinc SRC                                                      # SRC = SRC + 1   (A still holds it)\n\tgetB TWELFTH                                                 # B = the address just past the twelfth digit\n\tif_neq ADD_COLUMN                                            # not all twelve yet\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1: step over the bank's 0\n\tgoto NEXT_BANK\n\n# ==== the carrying: turn the column sums into decimal digits ====\n#     P = RESULT_LAST; CARRY = 0\n#     loop:\n#         X = memory[P] + CARRY\n#         CARRY = 0\n#         while X >= 10: X = X - 10; CARRY = CARRY + 1         # X modulo 10, and X divided by 10\n#         memory[P] = X\n#         if P == FIRST_CELL: stop\n#         P = P - 1\n# After sub, the carry flag of the CPU is set when A >= B (no borrow). (That flag has nothing to do\n# with the variable CARRY.)\n@ ALL_BANKS_DONE\n\tlia RESULT_LAST; putA P                                      # P = the address of the last cell\n\tlia 0; putA CARRY                                            # CARRY = 0\n@ NEXT_CELL\n\tlia 0; getB P; load rgA rgB                                  # A = memory[P]\n\tgetB CARRY; sum; putA X                                      # X = memory[P] + CARRY\n\tlia 0; putA CARRY                                            # CARRY = 0\n@ TAKE_TEN\n\tgetA X; lib 10; sub                                          # A = X - 10\n\tif_carry TOOK_TEN                                            # X >= 10\n\tgetA X; putAat P                                             # memory[P] = X, now a single digit\n\tgetA P; getB FIRST_CELL                                      # A = P, B = the address of the first cell\n\tif_eq END_OF_PROGRAM                                         # the first cell is done\n\tlib 1; sub; putA P                                           # P = P - 1\n\tgoto NEXT_CELL\n@ TOOK_TEN\n\tputA X                                                       # X = X - 10   (A still holds the difference)\n\tinc CARRY                                                    # CARRY = CARRY + 1\n\tgoto TAKE_TEN\n\n# The word 0 stops the CPU on its Breakpoint.\n@ END_OF_PROGRAM\n\tnon\n\n# ---- The input: 200 banks ----\n# One word per battery; a 0 ends each bank; one more 0 ends the input.\norigin 0x2000\n# bank 1: 1252442221213212222222222222211212142351224112221222212213421221422124234123226223512212112521243121\n1; 2; 5; 2; 4; 4; 2; 2; 2; 1; 2; 1; 3; 2; 1; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 2; 1; 1; 2; 1; 2; 1; 4; 2; 3; 5; 1; 2; 2; 4; 1; 1; 2; 2; 2; 1; 2; 2; 2; 2; 1; 2; 2; 1; 3; 4; 2; 1; 2; 2; 1; 4; 2; 2; 1; 2; 4; 2; 3; 4; 1; 2; 3; 2; 2; 6; 2; 2; 3; 5; 1; 2; 2; 1; 2; 1; 1; 2; 5; 2; 1; 2; 4; 3; 1; 2; 1; 0\n# bank 2: 43324555833535423323435343342364